# CHANGELOG.waivers — deliberate rewrites of released changelog sections.
#
# `just check-changelog-history` asserts that every `## [x.y.z]` section in
# CHANGELOG.md still reads exactly as the `vx.y.z` tag shipped it, because a
# released section is a historical record and an edit to one is normally a
# mistake. This file is where the exception is written down.
#
# Written by `just changelog-waive <version> <reason>`, one line each:
#
#   <version> <sha256> # <reason>
#
# The hash covers the section's bytes AT THE MOMENT OF WAIVING, not the tag's,
# in the spirit of outdatty.lock beside it: a recorded hash is a review
# watermark — it means a human looked, not that a tool agreed. Recording one is
# therefore not a switch that turns the check off for a version. A later
# corruption of a waived section moves the bytes away from the recorded hash and
# fails the gate again, which the unconditional-skip spelling could not do.
#
# A waiver whose section has gone back to matching its tag fails too. An entry
# with nothing to cover is an entry nobody rereads, and the next edit to that
# section would land underneath it unreviewed.
#
# Empty of entries, and the shipped history has never been rewritten. If that
# stays true, this file stays a header.
